====

Client.put isolation (ISOLATION_MODE.BISECT) - known limitations, deferred
---------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Bisection can write a batch's entities out of order. Both halves of a
  rejected group are sent before either is isolated, so entities in a rejected
  first half are written after those in an accepted second half. If one put
  holds the same id twice, the earlier record can therefore overwrite the
  later one. INDIVIDUAL keeps the original order. Possible fixes: refuse (or
  isolate one at a time) a batch holding a repeated id, or isolate the first
  half before sending the second. Not urgent: current deliveries carry no ids,
  and the loader refuses a write holding a repeated id.

- The switch to one-at-a-time isolation lingers. The rejection-rate estimate
  (_RejectionTally) counts only entities in rejected batches, so clean batches
  never lower it: after one all-rejected batch, every later rejected batch in
  the same put is isolated one entity at a time even when its rejections are
  sparse. One reported sequence cost about 770 extra requests before switching
  back. Possible fixes: count every entity sent (clean batches included) in the
  estimate, or weight recent batches more heavily. The per-batch budget still
  bounds the cost at one request per entity plus 2*ceil(log2(batch size)).
